Here's email validation for a signup form:

**HTML + JavaScript (simple):**

```html
<form id="signupForm">
  <input 
    type="email" 
    id="email" 
    name="email" 
    required
    placeholder="Enter your email"
  />
  <button type="submit">Sign Up</button>
</form>

<script>
  document.getElementById('signupForm').addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const email = document.getElementById('email').value.trim();

    if (!email) {
      alert('Email is required');
      return;
    }

    const emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
    if (!emailRegex.test(email)) {
      alert('Please enter a valid email address');
      return;
    }

    // Form is valid, proceed with submission
    console.log('Form submitted with email:', email);
  });
</script>
```

**More robust version with error display:**

```javascript
function validateEmail(email) {
  // Basic format check
  const regex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;
  if (!regex.test(email)) {
    return { valid: false, error: 'Invalid email format' };
  }

  // Additional checks
  if (email.length > 254) {
    return { valid: false, error: 'Email is too long' };
  }

  const [localPart] = email.split('@');
  if (localPart.length > 64) {
    return { valid: false, error: 'Email local part is too long' };
  }

  return { valid: true, error: null };
}

// Usage
const result = validateEmail('user@example.com');
if (result.valid) {
  // Submit form
} else {
  // Show error: result.error
}
```

**Key points:**
- Use HTML5 `type="email"` for browser-level validation
- Always validate on the backend too (never trust client-side only)
- Keep the regex simple—RFC 5322 is complex; this pattern covers 99% of real emails
- Trim whitespace and check length limits

